From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: alpine-riscv32 <alpine-riscv32@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 22:58:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] clocksource: esp32s31-systimer: tick the boot CPU only

An SMP kernel with two CPUs in the device tree makes cpu_possible_mask
hold both. The systimer clockevent claims that mask, but its interrupt
reaches core 0 only and the interrupt chain has no irq_set_affinity, so
tick_check_percpu() refuses it as CPU 0's tick device. CPU 0 then ticks
from the riscv timer, whose MTIME stops while the core sits in WFI.

Claim CPU 0 alone, so the boot CPU keeps the systimer as its tick on an
SMP kernel. A second CPU still needs its own timer event.
---
 drivers/clocksource/timer-esp32s31-systimer.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/clocksource/timer-esp32s31-systimer.c b/drivers/clocksource/timer-esp32s31-systimer.c
index 8df1ca18..409f31bf 100644
--- a/drivers/clocksource/timer-esp32s31-systimer.c
+++ b/drivers/clocksource/timer-esp32s31-systimer.c
@@ -173,7 +173,14 @@ static int __init systimer_init(struct device_node *np)
 	systimer.ce.set_next_event	= systimer_set_next_event;
 	systimer.ce.set_state_shutdown	= systimer_shutdown;
 	systimer.ce.set_state_oneshot	= systimer_set_oneshot;
-	systimer.ce.cpumask		= cpu_possible_mask;
+	/*
+	 * Only the boot hart takes this interrupt: the interrupt matrix routes
+	 * it to core 0 and cannot move it. With two possible CPUs a device
+	 * whose mask covers both is refused as CPU 0's tick, because its IRQ
+	 * affinity cannot be set, and CPU 0 would fall back to the CLINT timer
+	 * that stops in WFI.
+	 */
+	systimer.ce.cpumask		= cpumask_of(0);
 	systimer.ce.irq			= irq;
 
 	ret = request_irq(irq, systimer_isr, IRQF_TIMER | IRQF_IRQPOLL,
-- 
2.43.0

